    "proof": "Condition (ii) immediately implies (i), since every fixed h is eventually below H_N. Conversely, under (i), for each m choose an integer N_m larger than N_(m-1) such that for every N>=N_m and 1<=|h|<=m one has |W_N(h)|<=1/m. Define H_N as the largest m with N_m<=N (and one before N_1). Then H_N is nondecreasing, tends to infinity, and the moving maximum is at most 1/H_N. The discrepancy conclusion is exactly the classical Weyl criterion, not a new arithmetic estimate. The complete rational grids replay simultaneous finite cancellation symbolically through the geometric root-sum identity; they contain no canonical Fermat-quotient data.",
